---
import BlogLayout from '../../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro';

const title       = 'Finding Wrong Labels with Geometry, Not a Model';
const description = 'geosift spots mislabelled data by looking at the shape of the data itself, so it stays reliable exactly where model-based cleaners fall apart: heavy, real-world noise. With two live demos.';
const date        = '2026-07-31';
const author      = 'hoofay';
const tags        = ['python', 'machine-learning', 'label-noise', 'clustering'];
const section     = 'datascience';

// Kept as a string constant so the LaTeX curly braces aren't parsed as JSX.
const bibtex = `@misc{hough2026geosift,
  title     = {Model-independent label-noise detection by feature geometry: pooled vs. local estimators},
  author    = {Hough, Daniel},
  year      = {2026},
  publisher = {Zenodo},
  doi       = {10.5281/zenodo.21627002},
  url       = {https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21627002}
}`;
---

<BlogLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  date={date}
  author={author}
  tags={tags}
  section={section}
>

  <p>
    Almost every real dataset has wrong labels in it. A crowd worker mis-clicks, an auto-labeller
    guesses badly, a scraped caption is nonsense. A few percent of a dataset being mislabelled is
    normal; a few tens of percent is common in crowdsourced or weakly-supervised data. Those bad
    labels quietly drag down every model you train on them, so it is worth finding and removing them.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="https://github.com/hoofay/geosift" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">geosift</a>
    is a small Python library that finds them. What makes it interesting is <em>how</em> it decides a
    label is wrong: it never trains a model to do the judging. It looks only at the geometry of the
    data, and that turns out to matter most exactly where the usual tools break.
  </p>

  <h2>The auditor problem</h2>

  <p>
    The standard way to catch bad labels is a method called <strong>Confident Learning</strong> (the
    <code>cleanlab</code> library). The recipe is sensible: train a classifier on your labelled data,
    then look for places where the trained model is confident the label is wrong. If the model is sure
    a picture is a dog but the label says cat, that label is suspicious.
  </p>

  <p>
    There is a catch, and it is circular. The model doing the checking was <em>itself trained on the
    same possibly-wrong labels</em>. When only a few labels are bad, the model mostly learns the true
    pattern and its complaints are trustworthy. But as the noise climbs, the model increasingly learns
    the mistakes themselves, and its "confident disagreements" start reflecting its own corruption. The
    memorable line from the paper: <em>the auditor has read the same record it is auditing.</em>
  </p>

  <p>
    So the tool you most need under heavy noise is the tool that degrades fastest under heavy noise.
  </p>

  <h2>Birds of a feather</h2>

  <p>
    geosift sidesteps the circle by throwing the model away. The idea rests on one everyday
    observation: things of the same kind tend to sit near each other. If you place every data point
    in "feature space" (dogs near dogs, cats near cats), then to guess a point's true class you can
    simply ask <em>what are its neighbours?</em> No training, no model to corrupt. A wrong label is
    then just a point whose neighbourhood disagrees with it: a lone "cat" sitting deep in a crowd of
    dogs.
  </p>

  <p>
    Crucially, the <em>neighbourhoods themselves</em> are built from positions only, never from the
    labels. Labels cannot corrupt something they were never used to make. That is the whole trick, and
    it is why the signal survives when a trained model would not.
  </p>

  <p>
    This isn't a brand-new idea. An earlier method, <strong>SimiFeat</strong>, already does training-free
    detection this way. geosift's actual contribution is narrower and more interesting: it changes
    <em>how big a neighbourhood you look at</em>, and it maps out exactly when that helps.
  </p>

  <h2>Two ways to look at a neighbourhood</h2>

  <p>
    There are two ways to read a point's surroundings, and they trade off against each other:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Local</strong> (SimiFeat's way): look at the handful of points nearest to your target,
      maybe its 10 nearest neighbours. Sharp and detailed, but it is a vote of only 10 possibly-wrong
      labels, so it is jumpy — high variance.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Pooled</strong> (geosift's way, the "forest of means"): group the data into broad regions
      of hundreds of points each, and read the class proportions of the <em>whole region</em>. Coarser,
      but the vote is now over hundreds of labels, so it barely wobbles — low variance.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The prediction that falls out of this: <strong>local wins when noise is light</strong> (detail
    matters, and 10 mostly-correct neighbours are plenty), while <strong>pooled wins when noise is
    heavy</strong> or clustered near class boundaries (a small neighbourhood is easily swamped with
    errors, but a region of hundreds is barely moved). The demo below lets you feel exactly that.
  </p>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────  LIVE DEMO 1  ───────────────────────── -->

  <h2>Demo 1: watch the small neighbourhood get fooled</h2>

  <p>
    Two classes, <span style="color:#FF6B8A;font-weight:600">pink</span> and
    <span style="color:#4FA3FF;font-weight:600">blue</span>, running live in your browser. Drag the
    <strong>noise</strong> slider to flip more and more labels at random. Click any point to inspect it.
    For the point you pick we draw two lenses: its <span style="color:#7CFC9E;font-weight:600">local
    neighbourhood</span> (the 10 nearest points) and its <span style="color:#BF5FFF;font-weight:600">pooled
    region</span> (its whole cluster). Each lens votes on what the point's label <em>should</em> be.
    Watch the local vote start flipping at high noise while the pooled vote holds steady.
  </p>

  <div id="gs-demo1" class="not-prose my-8 rounded-xl border border-datascience/30 bg-surface-raised p-5">
    <div class="flex flex-col md:flex-row gap-6">

      <div class="shrink-0">
        <canvas id="gs-canvas" width="360" height="320"
                class="rounded-lg border border-datascience/30 cursor-pointer"
                style="width:360px;height:320px;touch-action:none;background:#0A0A0F;"></canvas>
        <div class="mt-4 space-y-3 font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--color-text-secondary);">
          <label class="flex items-center gap-3">
            <span style="width:3.4rem">noise</span>
            <input id="gs-noise" type="range" min="0" max="60" step="1" value="15" class="flex-1" style="accent-color:#BF5FFF">
            <span id="gs-noisev" style="width:2.6rem;text-align:right;color:#BF5FFF">15%</span>
          </label>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="flex-1 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed" style="color:var(--color-text-secondary);">
        <div class="uppercase tracking-widest mb-2" style="color:var(--color-text-muted);">— the point you picked —</div>
        <div id="gs-truth" class="mb-4 p-3 rounded-lg" style="background:#0A0A0F;border:1px solid var(--color-surface-border);"></div>

        <div class="uppercase tracking-widest mb-2" style="color:#7CFC9E;">— local lens · 10 nearest —</div>
        <div id="gs-local" class="mb-4 p-3 rounded-lg" style="background:#0A0A0F;border:1px solid var(--color-surface-border);"></div>

        <div class="uppercase tracking-widest mb-2" style="color:#BF5FFF;">— pooled lens · whole region —</div>
        <div id="gs-pooled" class="p-3 rounded-lg" style="background:#0A0A0F;border:1px solid var(--color-surface-border);"></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-4 text-xs font-mono" style="color:var(--color-text-muted);">
      dots = labels (some now wrong) · <span style="color:#7CFC9E">green ring</span> = 10 nearest ·
      <span style="color:#BF5FFF">purple ring</span> = pooled region · click to pick a point
    </p>
  </div>

  <script>
    // ---- tiny deterministic PRNG so the scene is identical for everyone ----
    function mulberry32(a){return function(){a|=0;a=a+0x6D2B79F5|0;let t=Math.imul(a^a>>>15,1|a);t=t+Math.imul(t^t>>>7,61|t)^t;return((t^t>>>14)>>>0)/4294967296;};}

    const N = 170;
    const C = [[-1.15, 0.0], [1.15, 0.0]];   // two blob centres
    const SPREAD = 0.78;
    const COL = ['#FF6B8A', '#4FA3FF'];       // class 0 / class 1

    // build a fixed dataset: position + true class + a per-point flip-threshold
    const rnd = mulberry32(7);
    function gauss(){ // Box-Muller
      let u = 0, v = 0; while(u === 0) u = rnd(); while(v === 0) v = rnd();
      return Math.sqrt(-2*Math.log(u))*Math.cos(2*Math.PI*v);
    }
    const pts = [];
    for(let i=0;i<N;i++){
      const t = i < N/2 ? 0 : 1;
      pts.push({
        x: C[t][0] + gauss()*SPREAD,
        y: C[t][1] + gauss()*SPREAD,
        truth: t,
        flip: rnd(),          // label flips once noise% exceeds 100*flip
      });
    }

    // label-free k-means (k=2) on positions only — geometry, never labels
    function kmeans(){
      let cen = [[...C[0]], [...C[1]]];
      const assign = new Array(N).fill(0);
      for(let it=0; it<12; it++){
        for(let i=0;i<N;i++){
          const d0=(pts[i].x-cen[0][0])**2+(pts[i].y-cen[0][1])**2;
          const d1=(pts[i].x-cen[1][0])**2+(pts[i].y-cen[1][1])**2;
          assign[i]= d0<=d1?0:1;
        }
        const sum=[[0,0,0],[0,0,0]];
        for(let i=0;i<N;i++){const c=assign[i];sum[c][0]+=pts[i].x;sum[c][1]+=pts[i].y;sum[c][2]++;}
        for(let c=0;c<2;c++) if(sum[c][2]) cen[c]=[sum[c][0]/sum[c][2], sum[c][1]/sum[c][2]];
      }
      return {cen, assign};
    }
    const KM = kmeans();

    const SW=360, SH=320, VX=3, VY=2.6;
    const cv = document.getElementById('gs-canvas');
    const cx = cv.getContext('2d');
    const px = (x)=>((x+VX)/(2*VX))*SW;
    const py = (y)=>((VY-y)/(2*VY))*SH;

    let noise = 0.15;
    let sel = 0;    // selected point index

    // observed (possibly flipped) label of point i at the current noise level
    const obs = (i)=> pts[i].flip < noise ? 1-pts[i].truth : pts[i].truth;

    // pick a default point: a genuine pink one still inside the pink cluster but
    // as close to the boundary as possible — its small neighbourhood tips under
    // heavy noise while its pooled region holds, which is the whole point.
    (function(){
      let best=-1, bx=-9;
      for(let i=0;i<N;i++) if(pts[i].truth===0 && KM.assign[i]===0 && pts[i].x>bx){bx=pts[i].x;best=i;}
      sel = best;
    })();

    function nearest(i, k){
      const d=[];
      for(let j=0;j<N;j++) if(j!==i) d.push([(pts[i].x-pts[j].x)**2+(pts[i].y-pts[j].y)**2, j]);
      d.sort((a,b)=>a[0]-b[0]);
      return {idx: d.slice(0,k).map(z=>z[1]), r: Math.sqrt(d[k-1][0])};
    }

    function bar(pink, blue){
      const tot = Math.max(1, pink+blue);
      const pp = Math.round(100*pink/tot), bp = 100-pp;
      return `<div style="display:flex;height:9px;border-radius:5px;overflow:hidden;margin:6px 0">`+
             `<div style="width:${pp}%;background:#FF6B8A"></div>`+
             `<div style="width:${bp}%;background:#4FA3FF"></div></div>`;
    }

    function draw(){
      cx.clearRect(0,0,SW,SH);

      const nn = nearest(sel, 10);
      const myCluster = KM.assign[sel];

      // pooled region: translucent circle over the selected point's cluster
      let mcx=0,mcy=0,mc=0,maxd=0;
      for(let i=0;i<N;i++) if(KM.assign[i]===myCluster){mcx+=pts[i].x;mcy+=pts[i].y;mc++;}
      mcx/=mc; mcy/=mc;
      for(let i=0;i<N;i++) if(KM.assign[i]===myCluster){const d=Math.sqrt((pts[i].x-mcx)**2+(pts[i].y-mcy)**2); if(d>maxd)maxd=d;}
      cx.beginPath();
      cx.arc(px(mcx),py(mcy), (maxd/(2*VX))*SW*0.92, 0, Math.PI*2);
      cx.fillStyle='rgba(191,95,255,0.10)';
      cx.strokeStyle='rgba(191,95,255,0.55)';
      cx.lineWidth=1.5; cx.fill(); cx.stroke();

      // local region: circle out to the 10th nearest neighbour
      cx.beginPath();
      cx.arc(px(pts[sel].x),py(pts[sel].y), (nn.r/(2*VX))*SW, 0, Math.PI*2);
      cx.fillStyle='rgba(124,252,158,0.08)';
      cx.strokeStyle='rgba(124,252,158,0.7)';
      cx.lineWidth=1.5; cx.fill(); cx.stroke();

      // points, coloured by their (possibly wrong) observed label
      for(let i=0;i<N;i++){
        cx.beginPath();
        cx.arc(px(pts[i].x),py(pts[i].y), 3.4, 0, Math.PI*2);
        cx.fillStyle=COL[obs(i)];
        cx.globalAlpha = (i===sel)?1:0.85;
        cx.fill();
        cx.globalAlpha = 1;
      }

      // selected point marker
      cx.beginPath();
      cx.arc(px(pts[sel].x),py(pts[sel].y), 6.5, 0, Math.PI*2);
      cx.lineWidth=2.5; cx.strokeStyle='#fff'; cx.stroke();

      // ---- readouts ----
      const oLab = obs(sel), tLab = pts[sel].truth;
      const nameOf = (c)=> c===0 ? 'PINK' : 'BLUE';
      const misl = oLab !== tLab;

      document.getElementById('gs-truth').innerHTML =
        `label says: <span style="color:${COL[oLab]}">${nameOf(oLab)}</span>` +
        (misl ? `  &nbsp;<span style="color:#FFB454">(this one is actually ${nameOf(tLab)} — a real mislabel)</span>`
               : `  &nbsp;<span style="color:var(--color-text-muted)">(genuinely ${nameOf(tLab)})</span>`);

      // local vote
      let lp=0, lb=0; nn.idx.forEach(j=> obs(j)===0 ? lp++ : lb++);
      const lPred = lp>=lb ? 0 : 1;
      document.getElementById('gs-local').innerHTML =
        `${lp} pink vs ${lb} blue` + bar(lp,lb) +
        `verdict: should be <span style="color:${COL[lPred]}">${nameOf(lPred)}</span> ` +
        (lPred===tLab ? `<span style="color:#7CFC9E">✓ right</span>`
                      : `<span style="color:#FF5C5C">✗ fooled by noise</span>`);

      // pooled vote
      let pp=0, pb=0;
      for(let i=0;i<N;i++) if(KM.assign[i]===myCluster){ obs(i)===0 ? pp++ : pb++; }
      const pPred = pp>=pb ? 0 : 1;
      document.getElementById('gs-pooled').innerHTML =
        `${pp} pink vs ${pb} blue &nbsp;<span style="color:var(--color-text-muted)">(${pp+pb} points)</span>` + bar(pp,pb) +
        `verdict: should be <span style="color:${COL[pPred]}">${nameOf(pPred)}</span> ` +
        (pPred===tLab ? `<span style="color:#7CFC9E">✓ right</span>`
                      : `<span style="color:#FF5C5C">✗ fooled by noise</span>`);
    }

    document.getElementById('gs-noise').addEventListener('input', (e)=>{
      noise = (+e.target.value)/100;
      document.getElementById('gs-noisev').textContent = e.target.value + '%';
      draw();
    });
    function pick(ev){
      const r = cv.getBoundingClientRect();
      const mx = ((ev.touches?ev.touches[0].clientX:ev.clientX)-r.left)/r.width*SW;
      const my = ((ev.touches?ev.touches[0].clientY:ev.clientY)-r.top)/r.height*SH;
      let best=-1, bd=1e9;
      for(let i=0;i<N;i++){const d=(px(pts[i].x)-mx)**2+(py(pts[i].y)-my)**2; if(d<bd){bd=d;best=i;}}
      sel=best; draw();
    }
    cv.addEventListener('mousedown', pick);
    cv.addEventListener('touchstart', (e)=>{e.preventDefault();pick(e);}, {passive:false});
    draw();
  </script>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────  END DEMO 1  ───────────────────────── -->

  <p>
    Try this: pick the highlighted pink point near the boundary and slide the noise up past 40%. The
    green local vote — only 10 labels — swings around and eventually calls a genuinely-pink point blue.
    The purple pooled vote, counting hundreds of labels, keeps pointing at pink long after. That is the
    whole argument in one picture: <strong>a small sample is easy to poison; a big one is not</strong>.
  </p>

  <p>
    (Two honest notes. This toy uses random symmetric noise so you can see the mechanism; the real
    advantage is largest under <em>boundary-concentrated</em> noise, which is messier to animate. And
    at low noise you will see the local lens is perfectly reliable too — that is exactly the regime
    where it is actually the better choice.)
  </p>

  <h2>Demo 2: the crossover, with the real numbers</h2>

  <p>
    So who actually wins? The paper measures both detectors on ten datasets across three kinds of
    label noise, scoring each by <strong>F1</strong> against the known list of flipped labels (higher is
    better — it balances catching real errors against false accusations). The chart below shows the
    measured F1 for <span style="color:#BF5FFF;font-weight:600">geosift (pooled)</span> versus
    <span style="color:#8CA0B8;font-weight:600">SimiFeat (local)</span>. Toggle between 20% and 40%
    noise and watch the bars swap places.
  </p>

  <div id="gs-demo2" class="not-prose my-8 rounded-xl border border-datascience/30 bg-surface-raised p-5">
    <div class="flex gap-2 mb-4 font-mono text-xs">
      <button id="gs-b20" class="px-3 py-1.5 rounded-md border transition-colors" style="border-color:var(--color-surface-border);color:var(--color-text-secondary)">20% noise</button>
      <button id="gs-b40" class="px-3 py-1.5 rounded-md border transition-colors" style="border-color:var(--color-surface-border);color:var(--color-text-secondary)">40% noise</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="gs-bars" width="620" height="300" style="width:100%;max-width:620px;height:auto;background:#0A0A0F;border-radius:0.5rem;border:1px solid var(--color-surface-border)"></canvas>
    <p id="gs-caption" class="mt-3 text-xs font-mono" style="color:var(--color-text-muted);"></p>
  </div>

  <script>
    // Real F1 numbers from the paper (§5.2, voting detector). [pooled/geosift, local/SimiFeat]
    const DATA = {
      20: { title:'20% of labels wrong',
            note:'At light-to-moderate noise, local detail wins on every noise type.',
            groups:[
              {name:'symmetric',  f:[0.622,0.736]},
              {name:'asymmetric', f:[0.615,0.711]},
              {name:'boundary',   f:[0.463,0.467]},
            ]},
      40: { title:'40% of labels wrong',
            note:'At heavy noise the pooled method overtakes on structured & boundary noise — the crossover.',
            groups:[
              {name:'symmetric',  f:[0.740,0.744]},
              {name:'asymmetric', f:[0.677,0.618]},
              {name:'boundary',   f:[0.627,0.582]},
            ]},
    };
    const PURPLE='#BF5FFF', SLATE='#8CA0B8';
    const bc = document.getElementById('gs-bars');
    const bx = bc.getContext('2d');
    const BW=620, BH=300, PAD=46, TOP=24, BOT=54;
    let cur = 40;
    // animated heights
    let anim = DATA[40].groups.map(g=>g.f.map(()=>0));

    function drawBars(){
      bx.clearRect(0,0,BW,BH);
      const plotH = BH-TOP-BOT, plotW = BW-PAD-16, x0=PAD, y0=BH-BOT;
      const yMax=0.8;
      // gridlines + y labels
      bx.strokeStyle='rgba(255,255,255,0.08)';
      bx.fillStyle='#6b7280'; bx.font='11px monospace'; bx.textAlign='right';
      for(let v=0; v<=0.8+1e-9; v+=0.2){
        const y=y0-(v/yMax)*plotH;
        bx.beginPath(); bx.moveTo(x0,y); bx.lineTo(x0+plotW,y); bx.stroke();
        bx.fillText(v.toFixed(1), x0-8, y+4);
      }
      const groups=DATA[cur].groups, gW=plotW/groups.length;
      groups.forEach((g,gi)=>{
        const gx=x0+gi*gW;
        const barW=gW*0.26, gap=gW*0.08, cenx=gx+gW/2;
        [PURPLE,SLATE].forEach((col,bi)=>{
          const h=(anim[gi][bi]/yMax)*plotH;
          const bxpos = cenx + (bi===0 ? -barW-gap/2 : gap/2);
          bx.fillStyle=col;
          bx.fillRect(bxpos, y0-h, barW, h);
          // value label
          bx.fillStyle=col; bx.font='bold 12px monospace'; bx.textAlign='center';
          bx.fillText(g.f[bi].toFixed(3), bxpos+barW/2, y0-h-7);
        });
        // group label + winner tick
        bx.fillStyle='#c7ced9'; bx.font='12px monospace'; bx.textAlign='center';
        bx.fillText(g.name, cenx, y0+20);
        const winner = g.f[0] > g.f[1] ? 'geosift wins' : (g.f[1]>g.f[0] ? 'local wins' : 'tie');
        bx.fillStyle = g.f[0] > g.f[1] ? PURPLE : (g.f[1]>g.f[0] ? SLATE : '#6b7280');
        bx.font='10px monospace';
        bx.fillText(winner, cenx, y0+36);
      });
      // legend
      bx.textAlign='left'; bx.font='11px monospace';
      bx.fillStyle=PURPLE; bx.fillRect(x0, 10, 10,10); bx.fillStyle='#c7ced9'; bx.fillText('geosift (pooled)', x0+16, 19);
      bx.fillStyle=SLATE;  bx.fillRect(x0+150,10,10,10); bx.fillStyle='#c7ced9'; bx.fillText('SimiFeat (local)', x0+166,19);
    }

    function animateTo(target){
      const goal = DATA[target].groups.map(g=>g.f);
      cur = target;
      let step=0;
      const iv=setInterval(()=>{
        step++; const t=Math.min(1, step/18);
        anim = anim.map((row,gi)=> row.map((_,bi)=> goal[gi][bi]*t));
        drawBars();
        if(t>=1) clearInterval(iv);
      }, 16);
      document.getElementById('gs-caption').textContent = DATA[target].title + ' — ' + DATA[target].note;
      const on='#BF5FFF', off='var(--color-surface-border)';
      document.getElementById('gs-b20').style.borderColor = target===20?on:off;
      document.getElementById('gs-b20').style.color = target===20?'#BF5FFF':'var(--color-text-secondary)';
      document.getElementById('gs-b40').style.borderColor = target===40?on:off;
      document.getElementById('gs-b40').style.color = target===40?'#BF5FFF':'var(--color-text-secondary)';
    }
    document.getElementById('gs-b20').addEventListener('click', ()=>animateTo(20));
    document.getElementById('gs-b40').addEventListener('click', ()=>animateTo(40));
    animateTo(40);
  </script>

  <p>
    At <strong>20% noise the local method wins across the board</strong> — its finer resolution pays
    off and there aren't enough errors to swamp a small neighbourhood. At <strong>40% the picture
    flips</strong>: on asymmetric (structured) and boundary noise, the pooled forest pulls ahead,
    because those are exactly the conditions that poison a small neighbourhood while leaving a big
    region intact. ("Boundary" noise is the realistic hard case — the errors pile up right where two
    classes meet.)
  </p>

  <p>
    Averaged over everything, SimiFeat is still the marginally stronger single method. geosift is not a
    replacement; it is a <strong>specialist for the heavy-noise regime</strong>, and it knows it.
  </p>

  <h2>Does it hold on real mistakes?</h2>

  <p>
    Synthetic noise is a fair test bench, but the real prize is <em>genuine human error</em>.
    <strong>CIFAR-10N</strong> is a version of the classic CIFAR-10 image set re-labelled by real crowd
    workers; its "worst" split carries about 40% real mislabels — squarely in the regime where pooling
    should win. Running every detector on CLIP image embeddings (where similar images naturally cluster
    together), the head-to-head F1:
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-6 space-y-2 font-mono text-sm">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
